<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Víctor Raúl Haya de la Torre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Víctor Raúl Haya de la Torre was a Peruvian political leader who founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-popular-revolutionary-alliance">APRA</a> in 1924 as a continental anti-imperialist movement and led it for more than half a century. Repeatedly imprisoned or exiled, and barred by the army from taking power, he spent five years in asylum in the Colombian embassy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>. He presided over the Constituent Assembly that drafted the constitution of 1979 and died that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/victor-raul-haya-de-la-torre.jpg" alt="Víctor Raúl Haya de la Torre" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:V%C3%ADctor_Ra%C3%BAl_Haya_de_la_Torre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Haya de la Torre was born in Trujillo on 22 February 1895 into an aristocratic family. At the National University of Trujillo, where he studied literature from 1913, he befriended the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesar-vallejo">César Vallejo</a> in the circle later called the North Group. In 1919 he supported the workers' campaign for the eight-hour day and was elected president of the Peruvian Student Federation, and in 1922 the González Prada popular universities, night schools for workers, took shape. Banished by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-b-leguia">Augusto B. Leguía</a>, he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> on 16 November 1923 as secretary to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-vasconcelos">José Vasconcelos</a>, and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-popular-revolutionary-alliance">APRA</a> there on 7 May 1924.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Returning in 1930, he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-popular-revolutionary-alliance">Peruvian Aprista Party</a> and lost the 1931 presidential election to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-sanchez-cerro">Luis Miguel Sánchez Cerro</a>. He was arrested, and an APRA rising in Trujillo in 1932 was harshly repressed: over 6,000 were executed before the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chan-chan">Chan Chan</a>. The party was banned, and after an APRA member assassinated Sánchez Cerro on 30 April 1933 Haya lived in hiding until the party returned to legality in 1945, when it backed José Luis Bustamante y Rivero. After Manuel A. Odría's coup of 1948 he took refuge in the Colombian embassy, a case that became a reference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>, and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in 1954.</p><p>In 1956 he backed Manuel Prado Ugarteche, beginning the so-called coexistence, in which a party once clearly anti-oligarchic supported an oligarchic regime. He came first in 1962 with 33 per cent, but the armed forces vetoed him and overthrew Prado; in 1963 he lost to Fernando Belaúnde, and his party's alliance with Odría then controlled Congress and limited Belaúnde's agrarian reform. Under the military government he trained young leaders, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alan-garcia">Alan García</a>. Elected to the Constituent Assembly of 1978 with over one million votes, he presided over it for a salary of one sol de oro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Suffering from lung cancer, Haya signed the constitution on his deathbed on 12 July 1979 and died on 2 August at his home in the Ate District. He is buried in Trujillo under a rock inscribed Here lies the light. His thought began close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a> but parted from Leninism: he held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> was the highest stage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>, that capitalism was an inevitable phase that had to mature before it could be surpassed, and that the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> could not yet make that revolution.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Víctor Raúl Haya de la Torre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Politics in the following decades turned on the conflict between the oligarchy, the army and the mass party APRA, founded by <b>Víctor Raúl Haya de la Torre</b> in 1924, which was repeatedly banned.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trujillo-peru">Trujillo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-8.11,-79.03&amp;year=1949" aria-label="Trujillo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Víctor Raúl Haya de la Torre"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5923 5691h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5923 5691h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-8.11,-79.03&amp;year=1949">What was here in 1949? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
